<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gaoche</h1><p class="st-meta">487 CE – 541 CE (approx.) · capital Turpan region</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the Tiele 'high cart' tribes north-west of Turpan, which broke away from the Rouran in 487 and fought them for half a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Gaoche, named by the Chinese for their high-wheeled carts, were Turkic-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> tribes. In 487 their leader Afuzhiluo refused to join a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> campaign and led some 100,000 households west to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungaria</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, where he proclaimed an independent kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Gaoche were nomadic herders organised in clans under a king. They allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> against their common enemy the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran</a>, and competed for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and the oasis routes. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalites</a> intervened in their succession disputes, installing rulers of their choosing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After decades of war, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran</a> killed the Gaoche king Mi'etu in 516, and around 541 they finally destroyed the kingdom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> tribes later came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire</a>, and groups descended from them included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gaoche.jpg" alt="Gaoche" width="320" height="138"><figcaption>Image: Author: Benjamin Banayan (rugrabbit.com), photographed at the Metropolitan Museum of Art exhibit &quot;COURT AND COSMOS: The Great Age … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Court_of_Seljuk_ruler_Tughril_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>487 CE – 541 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turpan region</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Afuzhiluo, Mi'etu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gaoju, Tiele kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=514&amp;focus=gaoche" aria-label="Gaoche on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 981 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gaoche lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7402 767h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7738 1237h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7738 1237h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=514&amp;focus=gaoche">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
